{"name":"Blanched Thyme","hex":"#949579","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blanched","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["lacking complexion or color","bleached"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of blanch"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blanched"},{"term":"thyme","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant of the labiate genus Thymus, such as garden thyme (Thymus vulgaris), a warm, pungent aromatic, that is much used to give a relish to seasoning and soups."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/thyme"}],"entries":[{"title":"Thyme","blocks":[{"text":"Thyme is a culinary herb consisting of the dried aerial parts of some members of the genus Thymus of flowering plants in the mint family Lamiaceae. Thymes are native to Mediterranean countries in North Africa, Levant and Southern Europe. Thymes have culinary, medicinal, and ornamental uses. The species most commonly cultivated and used for culinary purposes is Thymus vulgaris, native to Southeast Europe."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Thyme-Bundle.jpg/330px-Thyme-Bundle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thyme","wikidata":"Q3215980"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Flowering_thyme.JPG/500px-Flowering_thyme.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Flowering_thyme.JPG/1280px-Flowering_thyme.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Flowering_thyme.JPG/500px-Flowering_thyme.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Flowering thyme","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlowering_thyme.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wiki